            </fr:date>
            <fr:anchor>1393</fr:anchor>
            <fr:addr type="user">sss-001R</fr:addr>
            <fr:route>sss-001R.xml</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="FollowLast Definition is Actually Fine">FollowLast Definition is Actually Fine</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Daily</fr:taxon>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <fr:p><fr:link href="sss-001Q.xml" title="Wrong Definition of FollowLast" addr="sss-001Q" type="local">Here</fr:link>, I mention that the definition of follow last sets in the Agda formalization needs to be changed to match the definitions used in <fr:link href="krishnaswami_typed_2019.xml" title="A typed, algebraic approach to parsing" addr="krishnaswami_typed_2019" type="local">[krishnaswami_typed_2019]</fr:link> and <fr:link href="bruggemannkleinwood.xml" title="Deterministic regular languages" addr="bruggemannkleinwood" type="local">[bruggemannkleinwood]</fr:link>.</fr:p>
            <fr:p>Instead I had define follow last sets as</fr:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:date>
                  <fr:year>2025</fr:year>
                  <fr:month>1</fr:month>
                  <fr:day>24</fr:day>
                </fr:date>
                <fr:anchor>580</fr:anchor>
                <fr:addr type="user">sss-0010</fr:addr>
                <fr:route>sss-0010.xml</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="FollowLast Set">FollowLast Set</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <fr:p>The followlast set (<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {FLast}(\cdot )]]></fr:tex>) of a grammar <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> are all the characters that may follow a word in the language of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> in a string that is in the language of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex>.

  <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[ \mathsf {FLast}(A) = \{ c ~|~ \exists  w , w_A . w_A \in  L(A) \land  w_Acw \in  L(A) \}]]></fr:tex></fr:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <fr:p>This definition is encoded in Dependent Lambek Calculus as follows,</fr:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors>
                  <fr:author>
                    <fr:link href="stevenschaefer.xml" title="Steven Schaefer" addr="stevenschaefer" type="local">Steven Schaefer</fr:link>
                  </fr:author>
                </fr:authors>
                <fr:date>
                  <fr:year>2025</fr:year>
                  <fr:month>1</fr:month>
                  <fr:day>24</fr:day>
                </fr:date>
                <fr:anchor>581</fr:anchor>
                <fr:addr type="user">sss-0011</fr:addr>
                <fr:route>sss-0011.xml</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="FollowLast Sets in Dependent Lambek Calculus">FollowLast Sets in Dependent Lambek Calculus</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <fr:p>The <fr:link href="sss-0010.xml" title="FollowLast Set" addr="sss-0010" type="local">followlast set</fr:link> of a grammar <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> may be captured in Dependent Lambek Calculus via the following proposition:

  <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[ c \not  \in  \mathsf {FLast}(A) := A \And  (A \otimes  \texttt {"}c\texttt {"} \otimes  \top ) \vdash  \bot ]]></fr:tex></fr:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <fr:p>It turns out the original definitions I used aren't incorrect, instead the follow last set of a disjunction must be augmented. In <fr:link href="krishnaswami_typed_2019.xml" title="A typed, algebraic approach to parsing" addr="krishnaswami_typed_2019" type="local">[krishnaswami_typed_2019]</fr:link> and <fr:link href="bruggemannkleinwood.xml" title="Deterministic regular languages" addr="bruggemannkleinwood" type="local">[bruggemannkleinwood]</fr:link>, the follow last sets of a disjunction obey a very aesthetic equality,
<fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[\mathsf {FLast}(A \oplus  B) = \mathsf {FLast}(A) \cup  \mathsf {FLast}(B)]]></fr:tex></fr:p>
            <fr:p>However, with my augmented definition if either of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> or <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex> is nullable, the follow last set of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A \oplus  B]]></fr:tex> must also be unioned with <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {First}(A) \cup  \mathsf {First}(B)]]></fr:tex>.</fr:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
      </fr:mainmatter>
    </fr:tree>
